    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "The major axis of the ellipse is 10, so the distance from the center of the ellipse to each focus is $10/2=5.$ The minor axis is 8, so the distance from the center of the ellipse to each vertex on the minor axis is $8/2=4.$ \n\nThe distance between a vertex and the corresponding focus is $5-4=1,$ so the major axis is 1 (this could also have been seen by drawing the rectangle with the normal height of the ellfipse and diagonal from one near vertex to the other near vertex, coloring in the area that overlaps the ellipse.) \n\nLet $r$ be the radius of the circle.  The circle is tangent to the ellipse, so $r$ must be larger than _a_ radius of the ellipse.  So the minimum value of $r$ is the radius of the ellipse minus a vertical distance from its major axis: $\\sqrt{5^2-1^2}-4=\\sqrt{25-1}-4=\\sqrt{24}-4=\\boxed{p}$\nhttp://resources.rushhourmath.com/math/math_formulas_pg-5.pdf\n\nwhat are the answers to this riddle were cut in half and still come out whole see box paint and see",
